I could not reproduce any cross-border outage. End-to-end reachability between the two endpoints is healthy: pc0 (20.1.1.10) can ping pc12 (120.1.1.10) and vice versa with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT (verified by topology-wide and targeted pings). Both hosts have correct default gateways configured (pc0 via 20.1.1.1; pc12 via 120.1.1.1). The “border post” (r11) is operating correctly: its interfaces are up (112.1.1.1/8 on eth0 toward r12, 111.1.1.2/8 on eth1 toward r1, 103.1.1.1/8 on eth2), OSPF is running on the 111.0.0.0/8 side, and EIGRP 100 on the 112.0.0.0/8 and 103.0.0.0/8 side. r11 is redistributing EIGRP into OSPF, and its routing table shows OSPF-learned routes for the 20.0.0.0/8 domain and EIGRP-learned routes toward the 120.0.0.0/8 side via r12. r1 also has OSPF routes for remote networks and directly connected 20.0.0.0/8, supporting the successful path to 120.1.1.10. r12 has 120.0.0.0/8 connected and a default route via 112.1.1.1, providing return reachability. Mechanism-wise, nothing is dropping in the middle; ICMP succeeds across the border. Root cause: not a network fault; the reported failure is not present at this time (possibly transient or application/DNS-related). Corrective fix: no network changes required. If issues recur, capture traceroute and timestamps or test the specific application/port from pc0 to 120.1.1.10 to isolate non-network causes. === END ===